    "reply": "I added `tests/test_payments.py` with 12 tests for `line_total`, `order_total` and `refund`. `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` passes, with one expected failure. I didn't change anything under `shop/`.\n\nThe tests cover:\n- **`line_total`:** normal, zero-quantity and fractional-price cases.\n- **`order_total`:** an empty order, tax applied, several lines summed before tax, rounding to two decimals, and `TAX_RATE` being used (the test patches it and restores it afterwards).\n- **`refund`:** partial, full and zero refunds.\n\n**Possible bug:** `refund()` doesn't check that the refund is no more than the order total. `refund(100, 150)` returns `-50`. I wrote a test expecting a `ValueError` and marked it `@unittest.expectedFailure`, as the skill requires. Once `refund()` rejects over-refunds, that test will start failing as an \"unexpected success\", and you can remove the marker.\n\nI picked `ValueError` as the error to expect. If you'd rather it raise something else, that test needs to change.",
